        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Issus</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">5 November 333 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">South of the ancient city of Issus, near present-day Iskenderun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on either side of a small river called the Pinarus; the identity of the river is disputed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Hellenic League under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, perhaps no more than 40,000 men including allies; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>, whose army is put at no more than 100,000 by most modern historians, against 250,000 to 600,000 in ancient sources</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Macedonian and allied troops defeated the Persian forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> fled, and Alexander captured his wife, daughters, mother and treasury.</span></div>
        </div>
