    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ancient sources put the Persian army at between 250,000 and 600,000 men, while most modern historians think it was no larger than 100,000 and Hans Delbrück gives as few as 25,000.</li><li>The battle was fought on a coastal plain only 2.6 km wide between the Gulf of Issus and the mountains, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> could not use his superiority in numbers.</li><li>It was the first time the Persian army had been defeated with the King present, and the battle has been called the beginning of the end of Persian power.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Where the Persian army was camped, the distance from the Gulf of Issus to the mountains is only 2.6 km, and the exact course of the Pinarus river there has been argued over for more than 80 years.</p></aside>
    </section>

