    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander crosses into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and defeats the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Battle of the Granicus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander occupies most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, taking coastal settlements such as Miletus and Halicarnassus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">333 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> marches north around the mountains, captures Issus and cuts Alexander's supply lines</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 333 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander's army defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> on the Pinarus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> flees</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iii">Agis III</a> defeats the Macedonian general Coragus in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Agis is finally defeated at the Battle of Megalopolis</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-issus" data-v="191e80e95a" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies draw up on either side of the river</b> Alexander placed the Companions on his right and the Thessalian cavalry on the left of the phalanx under Parmenion. Darius set his heavy cavalry by the coast, the Greek mercenaries next, and the Cardaces along the river and into the foothills.</li><li><b>The Persian cavalry charges across the river</b> The Persian horse crossed the river and charged Parmenion and the allied cavalry. The Thessalian cavalry faced a larger mass of heavy horse and charged and fell back by turns to buy time.</li><li><b>The Macedonian centre is thrown back</b> The centre phalanx advanced across the river and up the fortified bank and suffered badly against the mercenaries. The Macedonians were forced to retreat across the river.</li><li><b>The right wing breaks into the Persian line</b> Alexander led the hypaspists on foot against the Cardaces and punched a hole through the Persian line. The Agrianians drove back the skirmishers on his far right, which secured the flank of the Companions.</li><li><b>Alexander's cavalry attacks Darius, who flees</b> Alexander mounted a horse at the head of the Companions and led a direct assault on Darius and his bodyguards. They fled from the battlefield.</li><li><b>The Companions strike the mercenaries from behind</b> Alexander saw his left flank and centre in trouble and let Darius go. He crashed into the rear of the Greek mercenaries, who broke up and began to retreat.</li><li><b>The Persians abandon their positions</b> The Persians saw that the Great King had gone and that the battle was being lost, and they fled in full rout. The Hellenic cavalry pursued them for as long as there was light.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-iii.jpg" alt="Darius III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_III_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander set out into Asia in 334 BCE and defeated the local Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Battle of the Granicus</a>. He then occupied most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, with the idea of capturing all coastal settlements so as to negate the power of the vastly superior Persian fleet. He took several important settlements, among them Miletus in 334 BCE and Halicarnassus.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> took personal command of his army after the Granicus. While Alexander was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, he heard that Darius was massing a great army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. If Darius reached the Gulf of Issus, he could use the support of the Persian fleet under Pharnabazus, still operating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, which would ease his supply and possibly let him land troops behind the enemy. Alexander kept his main army at Tarsus but sent Parmenion ahead to occupy the coast around Issus. In November he received reports that the Persian army had advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to a town named Sochoi, and he decided to mass his scattered army and advance south from Issus through the Pass of Jonah.</p><p>Darius knew that Parmenion held the Pass of Jonah and chose a northern route. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> captured Issus without opposition and cut off the hands of all the sick and wounded that Alexander had left behind. Darius had now placed his army behind the Hellenic advance and cut its supply lines, and Alexander was forced to countermarch.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrian.jpg" alt="Arrian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrian7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WikiSysop, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Some ancient sources, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, who based their accounts on earlier Greek sources, estimated 600,000 Persian soldiers in total, while Diodorus and Justin estimated 400,000 and Curtius Rufus 250,000. Modern historians find Arrian's count highly unlikely, arguing that fielding more than 100,000 soldiers in battle was extremely difficult at the time. Hans Delbrück gives an estimate as small as 25,000, although most, including Engels and Green, put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a>' army at no more than 100,000, including 11,000 cavalry, 10,000 Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a> and 10,000 Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>.</p><p>The Hellenic army, led by Alexander, may not have exceeded 40,000 men including allies. It may have consisted of about 24,000 heavy infantry (9,000 phalangites, 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">hypaspists</a>, and 7,000 allied and 5,000 mercenary Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>), 13,000 light infantry (peltasts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and slingers) and 5,850 cavalry. Alexander led his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> on the right flank and set his Thessalian allied cavalry on the left of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> under Parmenion.</p><p>Darius formed his line with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> next to the coast on his right, then the Greek mercenary phalanx, which A. M. Devine puts at 12,000. Next to it he spread his Persian infantry, the Cardaces, along the river and into the foothills, where they wrapped round to the other bank and threatened Alexander's right flank. Arrian gives these troops an inflated figure of 20,000. Darius stood in the centre with the Greek mercenaries, his royal cavalry guard and his best infantry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Persian cavalry first charged Parmenion and the allied cavalry, crossing the river to open the battle. Things did not go well for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> at the start. Their centre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, having to advance across a river and up a fortified bank, suffered severely against the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> waiting on the other side. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> noted that a hundred and twenty Macedonians 'of note', probably officers, were killed there, and the Macedonians were forced to retreat across the river. On the left flank the Thessalians struggled against the larger mass of Persian heavy horse, charging and retreating again to buy time.</p><p>Then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">hypaspists</a>, led by Alexander on foot, attacked the Cardaces and punched a hole through the Persian line. The Agrianians drove back a mass of Persian skirmishers menacing Alexander's far right, which secured the flank of the Companions. Alexander then mounted a horse at the head of his Companions and led a direct assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> and his bodyguards, who fled from the battlefield.</p><p>Alexander saw that his left flank and centre were in trouble and, letting Darius go, crashed into the rear of the Greek mercenaries, who broke up and began to retreat as well. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> saw that their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a> had gone and that the battle was being lost, and they abandoned their positions and fled in full rout. The Hellenic cavalry pursued them for as long as there was light.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As with most ancient battles, significant carnage occurred after the fighting, as the pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> slaughtered their crowded, disorganised foe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> notes Ptolemy mentioning that, while pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a>, Alexander and his bodyguards came upon a ravine which they crossed easily on the piled-up bodies of dead <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>.</p><p>Darius fled, leaving behind his family and treasury, which Alexander captured. The Hellenes took Darius' wife Stateira I, his daughters Stateira II and Drypetis, and his mother Sisygambis, all of whom had accompanied him on the campaign. Alexander, who later married Stateira II, treated the captured women with great respect. It was the first time the Persian army had been defeated with the King present.</p><p>Later, the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iii">Agis III</a> recruited the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> survivors of the battle who had served in the Persian army, a force of 8,000 veterans, and used them in his fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>. In the summer of 331 BCE Agis defeated Coragus, the Macedonian general in command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and the garrison of Corinth, but he was finally defeated at the Battle of Megalopolis. Agis used the veterans of the Persian army in his fight against the Macedonians, a use of the survivors that followed directly from the defeat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several ancient writers are named for the battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> based their accounts on earlier Greek sources and gave 600,000 as the Persian total, Diodorus and Justin 400,000 and Curtius Rufus 250,000. Arrian also gives an inflated figure of 20,000 for the Cardaces, and he notes Ptolemy's mention of the ravine of bodies. Callisthenes' accounts of the measurements of the battlefield and of the distances marched by both armies, together with a distance given by Diodorus after the battle, are the evidence on which the site has been argued.</p><p>The site itself is disputed. Speculation on the location of the Pinarus has gone on for over 80 years. Older historians believed it to be the Deli Tchai river, but N. G. L. Hammond and A. M. Devine claim that it is actually the Payas River, Devine using his own examination of its course, which he considered would not have changed drastically since antiquity.</p><p>The motives of the two kings are also debated. One modern view, based on Curtius, is that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> was forced to move his camp to terrain that favoured Alexander, who was fighting defensively on the advice of his war council and Parmenion. In another reading Alexander waited at Myriandus to spring a trap on Darius in the narrow crossing of a pass, and Darius instead went north around the mountains, emerging behind Alexander's position and on his supply lines. On that reading Alexander was caught off guard and forced to march to Darius.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela.jpg" alt="Battle of Gaugamela" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_da_Cortona_-_Battle_of_Alexander_versus_Darius_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pietro da Cortona, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is called a decisive Hellenic victory that marked the beginning of the end of Persian power. It was the first time the Persian army had been defeated with the King present, and it was the second major battle of Alexander's invasion of the Persian empire and his first encounter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>. Darius, after fleeing, no longer had his family or his treasury, both of which were in Alexander's hands.</p><p>Its shape resembles that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> two years later, where Parmenion again held the left wing long enough against superior Persian numbers for Alexander to make his cavalry strike against Darius and break the Persian army. The present-day Turkish city of Iskenderun, close to ancient Issus, bears the Turkish equivalent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, a city Alexander founded to commemorate his victory.</p><p>The battle has been pictured by later artists. The German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painter and printmaker Albrecht Altdorfer made The Battle of Alexander at Issus in 1529, and Jan Brueghel the Elder painted The Battle of Issus in about 1599 to 1600; it hangs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Both works are dated well over a thousand years after the battle.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Issus on 5 November 333 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s army defeated the Persian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> on a narrow coastal plain in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Darius fled, and Alexander captured his family and treasury. Ancient and modern figures for the Persian army differ widely.</em></p>

